5. What is Nmap?

Nmap, short for Network Mapper, is a network scanning tool used to discover hosts and examine the ports and services available on a system.

It can be used to determine whether ports are open, closed, or filtered. Nmap also provides options for identifying services and attempting to determine the operating system of a target.

It is commonly used by security professionals and network administrators for network discovery, troubleshooting, and security assessments.

6. Why Network Scanning Matters

Network scanning is useful because it provides information about how a system is exposed to a network.

An open port can indicate that a service is listening for connections. If unnecessary or poorly secured services are exposed, they can increase the attack surface of a system.

Regular scanning can help administrators identify unexpected services, review firewall configurations, and check whether changes to a system have introduced new network exposure.

Network scanning should always be performed only on systems that are owned by the tester or where proper permission has been provided.

7. Scanning Methodology

Three main Nmap scans were performed against the primary target.

7.1 Basic Scan

The following command was used:

bash
nmap 192.168.163.129

The scan showed that the host was up.

Nmap scanned the default 1000 TCP ports. The results showed that the ports were filtered and no open ports were identified.

7.2 Service and Version Scan

The following command was used:

bash
nmap -sV 192.168.163.129


This scan was used to identify services and their versions on open ports.

The host was detected as up, but no open TCP ports were identified. Therefore, no running network services were identified by this scan.

7.3 OS Detection Scan

The following command was used:

bash
sudo nmap -O 192.168.163.129

The host was detected as up. However, Nmap could not provide specific operating system details because there were not enough fingerprints available from the scan.

7.4 Additional Localhost Scan

As an additional check, I performed a scan against the local loopback address:

bash
sudo nmap -sS -sV -O 127.0.0.1


The localhost was detected as up. All 1000 scanned TCP ports were reported as closed, and no open TCP ports were identified.

